Credential Access – LSASS Memory Dump Investigation Report
1. Executive Summary

An investigation was conducted into a suspected Credential Access activity involving the Windows Local Security Authority Subsystem Service (LSASS) process.

The investigation identified an attempt to duplicate a handle associated with lsass.exe. The activity was attributed to Outflank-Dumpert.exe. Further investigation showed that the process created a file named dumpert.dmp, which was identified as an LSASS memory dump.

Additional registry activity involving W32Time\SecureTimeLimits was subsequently observed, with Outflank-Dumpert.exe identified as the caller.

The observed sequence is consistent with LSASS memory dumping for credential access.

2. Investigation Objective

The objective of the investigation was to determine:

Whether lsass.exe was accessed by an unauthorized or suspicious process.
Which process attempted to obtain an LSASS handle.
Whether an LSASS memory dump was created.
Whether any additional activity occurred following the memory dump.
Whether the observed activity is consistent with credential-dumping behavior.
3. Investigation Methodology

Since the incident was related to LSASS, the investigation was initiated by searching the available logs using the keyword:

lsass.exe

Relevant events were then correlated based on:

Process name
Object name
Handle activity
File creation
Registry activity
Process relationships
4. LSASS Handle Duplication

The initial investigation identified an event indicating:

“An attempt was made to duplicate the handle of an object.”

Further examination of the associated event showed that the requested object was:

lsass.exe

The process associated with the handle-duplication activity was:

Outflank-Dumpert.exe

Finding

The activity indicates that Outflank-Dumpert.exe attempted to obtain/duplicate a handle associated with the LSASS process.



5. Outflank-Dumpert Process Analysis

Further analysis was performed on the executable responsible for the LSASS handle activity.

The identified image name was:

Outflank-Dumpert.exe

The executable reported the following Original File Name:

version.dll

The difference between the image name and reported original file name was documented as part of the process investigation.



6. LSASS Memory Dump Creation

Following the LSASS handle activity, the investigation identified the creation of:

dumpert.dmp

The dump file was associated with Outflank-Dumpert.exe, and the object/process context indicated that the dump was generated from lsass.exe.

The creation of an LSASS memory dump is significant because LSASS memory can contain sensitive authentication material.

Finding

The observed activity provides evidence of an LSASS memory-dumping operation.



7. Registry Activity

After the dump-file creation, additional registry activity was observed involving:

W32Time\SecureTimeLimits

The caller associated with this registry activity was:

Outflank-Dumpert.exe

This activity was documented as subsequent activity in the incident timeline.



